(7467853, 1185092, 103142, '<td valign="top">Look at that! No votes at 4 or under!<br/> <br/>I love the tufts on his head. :)<br/> <br/>Great capture. Bluebirds are so awesome! I haven''t had them around for years, but they were so wonderful...</td>', 'Look at that! No votes at 4 or under! I love the tufts on his head. :) Great capture. Bluebirds are so awesome! I haven''t had them around for years, but they were so wonderful...', '2016-08-10 00:04:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(7467878, 1185206, 103142, '<td valign="top">Nope. This wasn''t focus stacked. There was enough light to get it up to 6.3. It seems like f8 is where to be for good dof for bugs (so far). I''m just lucky that the robber flies are so incredibly interesting. My other bug shots didn''t go over as well. But it''s been fun. I need to play with this more often. :)</td>', 'Nope. This wasn''t focus stacked. There was enough light to get it up to 6.3. It seems like f8 is where to be for good dof for bugs (so far). I''m just lucky that the robber flies are so incredibly interesting. My other bug shots didn''t go over as well. But it''s been fun. I need to play with this more often. :)', '2016-08-10 06:10:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(7467895, 1184415, 83149, '<td valign="top">Wow, just a spectacular shot, and look at that score, it''s a nice little bump to your PB :)</td>', 'Wow, just a spectacular shot, and look at that score, it''s a nice little bump to your PB :)', '2016-08-10 09:02:28.000000', NULL, 0),
(7467903, 1185092, 9581, '<td valign="top">It is a great shot.</td>', 'It is a great shot.', '2016-08-10 10:47:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(7467931, 1184982, 156041, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by tate:</b></div><hr/><i> a crowd pleaser - no doubt. Can''t wait to see the settings / method on this one. Very nice. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Thank you for your nice comments. You requested an explanation of how this was created, here it is: The camera settings were f/2.8, 25 seconds with ISO of 2500. I copied the raw image into Lightroom (LR). I made a virtual copy of the image and used the Basic Panel in LR to process one copy to optimize the sky and the other copy to optimize the foreground and Lake. I then took both copies into Photoshop (PS) as layers and using a luminosity mask, blended the two copies together to utilize the sky from one and the foreground from the other. I then used 2 different lights luminosity masks to lighten the Milky Way; a lights luminosity mask to lighten the mountain; and a lights luminosity mask to slightly darken the reflection; a used a curves adjustment to lighten the image, covered it with a black mask and painted in those portions of the Milky Way that I wanted to lighten further; I used a rough selection of the mountain and lightened with a curves adjustment; I used a darks luminosity mask to darken portions of the sky and reflection; I used one color balance layer; I used Nik Color Efex Pro (Darken/Lighten Center) to create a slight vignette; I finished by merging all layers into one (Cmd/Option/Shift/e), performed noise reduction on that layer with the Imagemonic Noise Reduction Plug-in and then used a luminosity mask to apply the noise reduction only to the darkest parts of the image. Thats probably more than you want to know - but there it is. Feel free to follow-up with any questions that you might have.</td>', 'Originally posted by tate: a crowd pleaser - no doubt. Can''t wait to see the settings / method on this one. Very nice.   Thank you for your nice comments. You requested an explanation of how this was created, here it is: The camera settings were f/2.8, 25 seconds with ISO of 2500. I copied the raw image into Lightroom (LR). I made a virtual copy of the image and used the Basic Panel in LR to process one copy to optimize the sky and the other copy to optimize the foreground and Lake. I then took both copies into Photoshop (PS) as layers and using a luminosity mask, blended the two copies together to utilize the sky from one and the foreground from the other. I then used 2 different lights luminosity masks to lighten the Milky Way; a lights luminosity mask to lighten the mountain; and a lights luminosity mask to slightly darken the reflection; a used a curves adjustment to lighten the image, covered it with a black mask and painted in those portions of the Milky Way that I wanted to lighten further; I used a rough selection of the mountain and lightened with a curves adjustment; I used a darks luminosity mask to darken portions of the sky and reflection; I used one color balance layer; I used Nik Color Efex Pro (Darken/Lighten Center) to create a slight vignette; I finished by merging all layers into one (Cmd/Option/Shift/e), performed noise reduction on that layer with the Imagemonic Noise Reduction Plug-in and then used a luminosity mask to apply the noise reduction only to the darkest parts of the image. Thats probably more than you want to know - but there it is. Feel free to follow-up with any questions that you might have.', '2016-08-10 14:45:31.000000', NULL, 0),
